The function must accept strings built only from round and square brackets and report whether every closer matches the most recent unmatched opener of the same type. The empty string counts as valid.

The core of the implementation is a stack walk:

    closing_to_opening = {')': '(', ']': '['}
    stack = []
    for c in s:
        if c in '([':
            stack.append(c)
        elif c in closing_to_opening:
            if not stack or stack.pop() != closing_to_opening[c]:
                return False

Each opener is pushed. Each closer pops the top of the stack and compares it with the opener the mapping names for it; an empty stack at that moment, or a mismatched type, ends the scan with False. Any character outside the four allowed brackets also returns False through the trailing else branch. After the loop the function returns not stack, so an unclosed opener makes the result False, while an empty input never enters the loop and comes back True.

I traced '([)]', which none of the three examples covers. After two characters the stack holds '(' under '['. The third character ')' pops '[' and compares it with '(', so the function returns False, which is right because the square bracket was still open when the round one closed. The string '[(())]' pushes three openers and pops them in reverse order with matching types, giving True, and ')(' is rejected on its first character because nothing is on the stack yet. The rule is applied one character at a time for any length or nesting depth, with no reference to the example strings.

