A flat dictionary with separator-joined keys must be turned back into nested dictionaries: each key is split on sep, intermediate segments become (or reuse) nested dicts, and the last segment receives the value. An empty input gives an empty dict.

The body of the function is:

    for key, value in flat.items():
        parts = key.split(sep)
        node = root
        for part in parts[:-1]:
            if part not in node or not isinstance(node[part], dict):
                node[part] = {}
            node = node[part]
        node[parts[-1]] = value

For each entry the key is split, and a cursor descends from the root through every segment but the last, creating a dict where none exists and reusing one that does. The final segment is assigned the value at the deepest level. Because existing dicts are reused, several keys sharing a prefix end up under the same parent, which is the (or reuse) part of the spec. The isinstance guard also means a segment holding a plain value is replaced by a dict if a later key needs to descend through it.

The examples only go two levels deep and always use '.', so I tried unflatten_dict({'a.b.c': 1, 'a.d': 2}, '.'). The first key creates a, then b inside it, then sets c to 1; the second key reuses a and sets d to 2, giving {'a': {'b': {'c': 1}, 'd': 2}}. With the two-character separator '::' and keys 'k::v' and 'k::w::z', the result is {'k': {'v': 1, 'w': {'z': 2}}}, since split works on any separator string. The implementation is general.

